hints.content:
Notice the numerator is missing a $1^{\text{st}}$ degree term. Let's add it as $0x$.

$\begin{array}{r}
x\phantom{+2}
\\
x-1|\overline{x^2+0x+2}
\\
\mathllap{-(}\underline{x^2-\phantom{0}x\phantom{+2}\rlap )}
\\
x+2
\end{array}$

options.explanation:
We start by asking, "what can we multiply by $x-1$ to get $x^2+k$ (no $x$-term) where $k$ is an integer?"

The answer is $x+1$ (remember the difference of squares pattern):

$\blueD{(x-1)(x+1)=x^2-1}$

Now we rewrite the numerator and split the quotient:

$\begin{align}
\dfrac{x^2+2}{x-1}&=\dfrac{(\blueD{x^2-1})+3}{x-1}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{\blueD{x^2-1}}{x-1}+\dfrac{3}{x-1}
\end{align}$

Now we use the fact that $\blueD{(x-1)(x+1)=x^2-1}$ to rewrite the numerator of the first quotient and cancel common factors:

$\begin{align}
&\phantom{=}\dfrac{\blueD{x^2-1}}{x-1}+\dfrac{3}{x-1}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{\cancel{\blueD{(x-1)}}\blueD{(x+1)}}{\cancel{x-1}}+\dfrac{3}{x-1}
\\\\
&=x+1+\dfrac{3}{x-1}
\end{align}$
